.TH THINKFAN-NEXT 1 "October 2026" "thinkfan-next 1.0.0"
.SH NAME
thinkfan-next \- YAML-only Rust temperature-based fan controller
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B thinkfan-next
.RB [ \-c " FILE"]
.RB [ \-s " SECONDS"]
.RB [ \-b " BIAS"]
.RB [ \-nvdq ]
.RB [ \-p " [SECONDS]"]
.RB [ \-\-check | \-\-check-hardware ]
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RB [ \-\-once ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read configured temperatures and control Linux hwmon PWM or ThinkPad fans.
Always runs in the foreground. Use a service manager for background operation.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Only YAML is supported. The default is /etc/thinkfan.yaml.
No other configuration file is tried automatically.
The YAML mapping contains sensors, fans and global or per-fan levels.
Simple levels use [speed, lower, upper] arrays. Detailed levels use speed,
lower_limit and upper_limit mappings. Multiple fans require speed arrays
in global levels. Temperature units are degrees Celsius.
Optional control.down_delay_seconds (default 0) requires sustained cooling
before each single-level downgrade. Fans may override down_delay_seconds.
The control.prediction mapping configures rise_rate (0.4 Celsius/second),
rate_smoothing_seconds (2), bias_decay_seconds (25), max_bias (30 Celsius),
and max_sample_gap_seconds (120). Timing uses actual monotonic elapsed time.
Sensors may set read_timeout_seconds independently of data expiry (default:
twice the base interval). The daemon uses six workers partitioned into
fast reads (two), NVML, SMART, preparation and fan discovery. Repeated commands
may be coalesced; periodic mode/watchdog maintenance is preserved.
Sensors may set interval_seconds (0.1 to 86400) and max_age_seconds
(strictly greater than the interval; default twice the interval).
Expired required data requests each fan's configured final level. Cached readings do not count as
new acquisitions for rise prediction or continuous-cooling evidence.
Blocking reads occupy their worker partition; completion notifications wake
the main loop. Timed-out calls cannot be cancelled and no replacement workers
are created. Depulse is skipped when it would cross the next sampling deadline.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c , " --config FILE"
Use exactly this YAML configuration file, regardless of its extension.
.TP
.BR \-s , " --interval SECONDS"
Maximum sampling interval, 0.1 to 60 seconds. Default 5.
Rising temperature shortens the adaptive interval to at most 2 seconds.
Recovery adds one second per base interval of fresh acquisition time,
up to the configured maximum. Cached samples do not advance recovery.
.TP
.BR \-b , " --bias BIAS"
Signed rise prediction factor, -10 to 30. Default 0 (no bias).
The prediction horizon is BIAS/2 seconds, applied to the time-smoothed
temperature rise rate. Decay and amplitude limits are configured in YAML.
.TP
.BR \-d , " --no-disk-wakeup"
Skip sleeping SMART disks instead of waking them for a reading.
.TP
.BR \-p , " --depulse [SECONDS]"
Enable the ThinkPad pulsing-fan workaround. Bare -p uses 0.5 seconds.
.TP
.B --watchdog SECONDS
ThinkPad kernel watchdog timeout, 1 to 120 seconds. Default 120.
Must exceed the sampling interval plus depulse duration plus one second.
.TP
.B --check
Validate configuration without accessing hardware.
.TP
.B --check-hardware
Validate and read hardware without writing fan state.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Read inputs and print decisions without changing fans or watchdogs.
Routine summaries are coalesced to the base interval; state and target
changes are reported immediately. Use -v for every decision.
.TP
.B --once
Run one cycle, restore initial state and exit.
.TP
.B --lock-file PATH
Use this instance lock. Default /run/thinkfan-next.lock.
.TP
.BR \-n , " --foreground"
Stay in foreground, which is already the default.
.TP
.BR \-v , " --verbose"
Print every sample and decision; enable DEBUG logs. Repeat for TRACE.
.TP
.BR \-q , " --quiet"
Suppress the startup announcement. Diagnostics remain enabled.
.TP
.B --log-output auto|console|journal
Select the logging destination. Default auto verifies JOURNAL_STREAM against
stderr device and inode. Console forces human-readable stderr output;
journal sends structured events directly to journald.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Tracing filter directives, for example thinkfan_next=debug. Overrides the
verbosity default (INFO, DEBUG for -v, TRACE for -vv). Invalid directives
produce a warning and fall back to the verbosity default. Dry-run decisions
and USR1 snapshots use INFO and can be hidden by an explicit filter.
.SH LOGGING
Console logs include local date/time and UTC offset. Module paths are shown
with -v or for DEBUG/TRACE events. Level colors require a terminal on stderr
and an unset NO_COLOR environment variable.
Sample summaries label sensor channels and temperatures in Celsius.
Targets are ThinkPad levels or PWM values, not measured RPM. Startup waiting,
read failures, expiry and cooling delay have distinct labels. Predicted
temperatures are shown only when they differ from measured values.
Journal logs use tracing-journald with ERROR=3, WARN=4, INFO=6 and DEBUG/TRACE=7.
Event fields use the F_ prefix, such as F_SENSOR and F_BACKEND.
Journal initialization failure falls back to stderr with a warning.
Check/help/version output remains on stdout. Quiet mode suppresses only the
startup announcement.
.SH THINKPAD SETUP
ThinkPad fan writes require the thinkpad_acpi module parameter fan_control=1.
The supplied modprobe.d file sets this option when the module loads. Reboot after
installing it; rebuild the initramfs first if it loads the driver early.
For a built-in driver, use thinkpad_acpi.fan_control=1 on the kernel command line.
.SH SUSPEND INTEGRATION
Sleep handling is signal-driven; automatic logind monitoring is not implemented.
The systemd sleep helper signals only thinkfan-next.service, not processes
launched manually in a terminal. Such processes require separate sleep integration.
.SH SIGNALS
INT and TERM restore original fan state and exit. HUP reloads configuration;
invalid replacements leave the current configuration active. USR1 prints
temperatures, target speeds and worker statistics. PWR releases control and pauses; USR2 resumes control
with a bounded hardware recovery interval.
.SH ERRORS
Fatal runtime failures request each fan's configured final level and leave the protective state
in place. Writes may still fail if a driver or device is unavailable.
Optional inputs retain their array positions when unavailable. All inputs
unavailable is a fatal error. Required inputs in their retry budget cause
the configured final level until they recover.
If that command fails, PWM retains the unregulated full-speed fallback and
ThinkPad retains the automatic-mode fallback.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success or a clean signal exit; 1 on configuration, hardware or runtime
failure; 2 on command-line syntax errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
The examples and configuration reference shipped in the source repository.
Linux kernel hwmon and thinkpad-acpi documentation.
